Let  \[ g(x) =
\begin{cases}
-2x^2 & \text{if } x \geq 0,\\
x+5 & \text{if } x <0.
\end{cases}
\]Compute $g(g(g(g(g(2)))))$.

Express as a common fraction in simplest form: $$
\sqrt{12\frac{1}{9}}
$$

Let $x,$ $y,$ $z$ be nonnegative real numbers satisfying $x \leq 1$, $y \leq 2$, and $z \leq 3$. Define
\begin{align*}
C &= \sqrt{x + 3} + \sqrt{y + 6} + \sqrt{z + 12}, \\
D &= \sqrt{x + 1} + \sqrt{y + 2} + \sqrt{z + 3}.
\end{align*}
Find the minimum value of $C^2 - D^2.$

Compute all values of $b$ such that the following system has a solution $(x,y)$ in real numbers:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{xy} &= b^{2b}, \\
\log_b (x^{\log_b y}) + \log_b (y^{\log_b x}) &= 5b^5.
\end{align*}

Let \[g(x) =
\begin{cases}
3x - 4 &\text{if } x < 1, \\
x + 6 &\text{if } x \geq 1.
\end{cases}
\]Find $g(-3)$.

Find the value of $a$ for which the lines represented by the following equations are perpendicular:
\begin{align*}
y &= 2x + 13 \\
18y + ax &= -12.
\end{align*}

In a different magic square, the sum of the three entries in any row, column, or diagonal is the same value. The figure below shows four of the entries of a magic square. Find $y$.
[asy]
size(2cm);
for (int i=0; i<=3; ++i) draw((i,0)--(i,3)^^(0,i)--(3,i));
label("$y$",(0.5,2.5));label("$23$",(1.5,2.5));
label("$104$",(2.5,2.5));label("$5$",(0.5,1.5));
[/asy]

If $(x,y)$ is a solution to the system
\begin{align*}
xy &= 12, \\
x^2 y + xy^2 + x + y &= 99,
\end{align*}
find $x^2 + y^2$.

If the system of equations
\begin{align*}
4x - 2y &= a, \\
6y - 12x &= b.
\end{align*}
has a solution, find $\frac{a}{b},$ assuming $b \neq 0.$

Let $h(x)$ be a function defined as \[h(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
-x & x\le 0, \\
3x-30 & x>0.
\end{array}
\right.\] If $a$ is negative, find $a$ such that $h(h(h(6)))=h(h(h(a)))$.

Consider a grid of squares organized in 7 rows and 2 columns. The integers in each of the rows, in each of the columns, and in one of the diagonals from the top left to the bottom right form distinct arithmetic sequences. What is the value of $M$?

[asy]
unitsize(0.35inch);
draw((0,0)--(7,0)--(7,3)--(0,3)--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(1,3));
draw((2,0)--(2,3));
draw((3,0)--(3,3));
draw((4,0)--(4,3));
draw((5,0)--(5,3));
draw((6,0)--(6,3));
draw((6,4)--(7,4)--(7,-4)--(6,-4)--cycle);
draw((6,1)--(7,1));
draw((6,-1)--(7,-1));
draw((6,-2)--(7,-2));
draw((6,-3)--(7,-3));
label("32",(0.5,2.2),S);
label("10",(3.5,0.2),S);
label("16",(3.5,1.2),S);
label("$M$",(6.5,3.2),S);
label("8",(6.5,-3.2),S);
[/asy]

Below is a portion of the graph of a function, \( y=q(x) \):

[asy]
import graph; size(6cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-3.5,xmax=3.5,ymin=-4.25,ymax=6.25;

pen cqcqcq=rgb(0.75,0.75,0.75);
/*grid*/ pen gs=linewidth(0.7)+cqcqcq+linetype("2 2"); real gx=1,gy=1;
for(real i=ceil(xmin/gx)*gx;i<=floor(xmax/gx)*gx;i+=gx) draw((i,ymin)--(i,ymax),gs); for(real i=ceil(ymin/gy)*gy;i<=floor(ymax/gy)*gy;i+=gy) draw((xmin,i)--(xmax,i),gs);

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("",xmin,xmax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis("",ymin,ymax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true);
real f1(real x){return sgn(2x-2)*abs(2x-2)^(1/3)+2*sgn(2x-2)*abs(2x-2)^(1/5)+abs(2x-2)^(1/7);}
draw(graph(f1,xmin,xmax),linewidth(1));
clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);
label("$y=q(x)$",(xmax+0.25,f1(xmax)),E);
[/asy]

The value of \( q(-1) \) is an integer. What is it?

How many different routes are there from point $A$ to point $B$ if you can only move to the right or down along the drawn segments in a 3x3 grid?

[asy]
unitsize(0.08inch);
draw((0,0)--(15,0)--(15,15)--(0,15)--cycle);
draw((5,0)--(5,15));
draw((10,0)--(10,15));
draw((0,5)--(15,5));
draw((0,10)--(15,10));
dot((0,15));
dot((15,0));
label("$A$",(0,15),NW);
label("$B$",(15,0),SE);
[/asy]

Let \[g(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
x^2 - 2x + 1 &\text{ if } x > 3, \\
-x + 4 &\text{ if } -3 \le x \le 3, \\
5 &\text{ if } x < -3.
\end{array}
\right.\] Find $g(-4) + g(0) + g(4)$.

Julia, Kevin, and Lee are painting a long straight fence. 
Julia starts with the first picket and paints every $j$th picket; 
Kevin starts with the second picket and paints every $k$th picket; 
Lee starts with the third picket and paints every $l$th picket.
Define a positive integer $100j+10k+l$ as perfectly paintable when the triple $(j,k,l)$ of positive integers results in every picket being painted exactly once by only one of the painters. Find the sum of all perfectly paintable integers.

